<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benedetta of Cagliari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1232 or 1233</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benedetta was judge, or ruler, of the Judicate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> from 1214 until her death around 1232. Her reign was dominated by the growing power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, which in 1216 built the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-di-castro">Castel di Castro</a> on the hill above Cagliari. She struggled to preserve her independence through her marriages and appeals to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Benedetta was born around 1194, the daughter and heiress of William I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> and Adelasia, a daughter of Moroello Malaspina. On her father's death she was consecrated by Riccus, Archbishop of Cagliari, before the higher clergy and the grandees, and swore not to diminish the territory of the giudicato, alienate its castles or make foreign alliances without their consent. On 14 June 1214 she married Barisone III of Arborea, who took the dynastic name Torchitorio V, and the two ruled their giudicati jointly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Benedetta favoured natives over Pisans in her government and the Sardinian economy over that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Republic of Pisa</a>, and Pisa turned against her. Lambert Visconti, judge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a>, landed a large army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> and fortified the hilltop of S. Gilla, forcing her to flee to the interior. In 1217 his brother Ubald I Visconti made her surrender Cagliari and take it back as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> from the consul of Pisa. She and Barisone then allied with Comita III of Torres and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> to expel the Pisans.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-honorius-iii">Pope Honorius III</a> supported her, annulling in February 1217 the election of the Pisan Marianus to the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>. After Barisone's death that spring, Ubald arranged her marriage to the widowed Lambert, married on 9 April 1220, which the pope at once annulled. In December 1224 she renewed her homage to the Holy See, promising a yearly tribute of twenty pounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, no marriage without papal approval, and Cagliari to the pope should she die without heirs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>War resumed in 1226 with Lambert's heir, Ubaldo II. To protect herself she married twice more without papal permission, first Enrico di Ceola, a Pisan of the Capraia family, in 1227, then Rinaldo de Glandis. Violence drove her to the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-igia">Santa Igia</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a>, her ancestral home, where she died. Her son William reigned but never ruled, under the regency of her sister Agnes and Marianus of Torres, and by February 1233 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> had granted Massa away while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> was divided between the Pisan families of the Visconti, Capraia and Donoratico.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benedetta of Cagliari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pisa and Genoa competed for influence; in 1216 the Pisans built the fortress of Castel di Castro on the hill of Cagliari with Judge <b>Benedetta</b>'s reluctant consent.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-cagliari">Judicate of Cagliari</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-igia">Santa Igia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Massa Marittima, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.22,9.1&amp;year=1223" aria-label="Santa Igia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4775 758 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Benedetta of Cagliari"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5225 1028h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5225 1028h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.22,9.1&amp;year=1223">What was here in 1223? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
